From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 13:58:23 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] x86/boot: Read RDRAND and TSC from the CPUID registers that
 hold them

has_cpuflag() in the self-relocating kernel's KASLR reads
X86_FEATURE_RDRAND from CPUID leaf 1's EDX and X86_FEATURE_TSC from its
ECX. The two are the other way round: RDRAND is ECX bit 30 (capability
word 4) and TSC is EDX bit 4 (word 0). So the test for RDRAND reads a
reserved bit, and the test for TSC reads DS-CPL, which KVM does not
expose. kaslr_get_random_long() then mixes neither RDRAND nor RDTSC into
the virtual base, only the i8254 counter and a hash of boot_params,
where the decompressor's KASLR, from which this code was taken, mixes
both.

Read each flag from the register that holds it.  The virtual base is
then drawn from the decompressor's sources again, less its hash of the
build string, which is public.  The cost is one RDRAND and one RDTSC at
boot.

Carried by spin-stack/machine for #645 on top of Hou Wenlong's RFC
(lore cover.1769434279.git.houwenlong.hwl@antgroup.com, 2026-01-26),
whose 3/5 has the registers swapped.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/x86/boot/startup/kaslr.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/x86/boot/startup/kaslr.c b/arch/x86/boot/startup/kaslr.c
index 3083847..8114422 100644
--- a/arch/x86/boot/startup/kaslr.c
+++ b/arch/x86/boot/startup/kaslr.c
@@ -24,9 +24,9 @@ static inline bool has_cpuflag(int flag)
 
 	if (level >= 0x00000001) {
 		if (flag == X86_FEATURE_RDRAND)
-			reg = native_cpuid_edx(0x1);
-		else if (flag == X86_FEATURE_TSC)
 			reg = native_cpuid_ecx(0x1);
+		else if (flag == X86_FEATURE_TSC)
+			reg = native_cpuid_edx(0x1);
 	}
 
 	return test_bit(flag & 31, (unsigned long *)&reg);
